From: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Subject: [PATCH] media: qcom: camss: Add dual IFE pixel paths

A frame wider than one IFE line (3840 pixels on Titan 175) can be split
between two pixel paths, each fed by its own CSID crop of the same
CSIPHY stream. Link the second pixel path to the video node of the
first: that master owns the buffers, the slave writes its stripe at the
master's output width into them with the master's stride, and latches
together with it. The video node starts the slave chain first, up to
what it shares with the master chain, so the CSIPHY and the sensor start
last and both CSIDs take the first frame.

The two CSIDs use the IPP halt modes the vendor driver uses on Titan
175: the master CSID runs in master mode and resumes at a frame
boundary, the slave in slave mode naming the master, without a command
of its own, so it follows the master's resume and halt and the stripes
start and stop on the same frame. The roles come from the VFE pairing,
which the slave's pixel path sets up before its CSID starts.

The master applies each parameter block to the slave too, and splits the
AWB grid: its own columns over the left stripe, the others over the
slave's stripe after its crop, written after the master's records into
the same statistics buffer.

An image or a statistics buffer completes once both halves are written.
The halves are matched by completion time, within VFE_DUAL_MATCH_NS of
each other, rather than by count, so a frame one side skips (for want of
a buffer, or at the start) does not shift every later pair; a buffer
whose other half never came completes with VB2_BUF_STATE_ERROR. Without
a buffer the write masters skip a frame and write nothing.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Origin: local
Upstream-Status: Pending
